      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Percentage Calculation Mistakes</h2>
      <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Reversing Markup to Find Original:</strong> If a $100 item is discounted by 20% to $80, adding 20% of $80 does not recover $100 ($80 + $16 = $96). Always divide by the remaining decimal factor <code>(1 - Rate)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Confusing Proportions with Multipliers:</strong> Multiplying a number by 20% means finding one-fifth of that number. To increase a number by 20%, multiply by 1.20, not 0.20.</li>
        <li><strong>Dividing by Zero:</strong> When determining what percentage X is of Y, the denominator Y cannot be zero. Zero represents an empty whole, making the percentage ratio undefined.</li>
      </ul>
